# tstack workflow (skills first)

The closest AGENTS.md wins over this rule. This rule adds; it does not replace it.

## Before any tech task
1. Name the skills for the task and read each SKILL.md before you edit. An empty skill list does not mean none apply: look for SKILL.md files.
2. Pick from this table, at most four skills:

| Task | Load first |
|------|------------|
| Feature or bug fix | test-driven-development, principle-experience-first (feature) or systematic-debugging (bug) |
| Refactor or cleanup | principle-encode-lessons-in-structure, principle-boundary-discipline |
| Two fixes that disagree | principle-attack-the-premise |
| Any claim of done | verification-before-completion |
| Commit, PR, issue, public doc | public-host-alias |
| Long or multi-step work | tstack tomato-loop (time-boxed block), then harvest |
| Repo work from a plan | tstack pstack-map route, then poteto-mode |

3. Model per role comes from `pstack-models.mdc`.

## While working
- Branch and PR only. Never merge, force-push, rewrite history, tag or release.
- Merges are checked with `tstack/bin/merge-check` by the owner, not by you.
- Fixtures are fictional. Hosts are named laptop-1, laptop-2, mac-mini or box.
- No real hostnames, usernames, home paths, phone numbers or email addresses.
- New prose says SI, not AI.

## Report
- List the skills you actually loaded, as `skill: <name>` lines in the PR body.
- Add `proof: <link or path>` for each claim.
- Then: read only, ran locally, tests and CI, not run (always present).
